trading idea: bid/ask imbalance and short-term volume expansion predict returns, reflecting intraday liquidity fragmentation and retail-driven order splitting.
[hypothesis_agent] iteration: 0
[hypothesis_agent] response: {
  "hypothesis": "Within a 5-minute window, an extreme bid/ask-side imbalance in aggressive spread-crossing orders (SpreadOrder buy-vs-sell imbalance) coupled with a sudden expansion in total submitted order volume (OrderLine volume vs its recent baseline) predicts a short-horizon return continuation over the next 1–3 bars, but mean-reverts over the next 6–24 bars when the aggressive-order-to-trade conversion (SpreadOrder vs TradeLine “fill efficiency”) is low—reflecting liquidity fragmentation where retail-driven order splitting creates persistent order-flow pressure unless the imbalance is mostly unfilled/canceled (temporary pressure) which subsequently reverts.",
  "reason": "Microstructure mechanism: In fragmented intraday markets, price changes at very short horizons are driven by the imbalance of liquidity-taking flow (aggressive orders crossing the spread). When this aggressive imbalance arrives alongside a sharp expansion in overall order submissions, it indicates a demand/supply shock that is being expressed through many small child orders (order splitting), which tends to persist for several minutes because execution is distributed across venues and time. This persistence yields short-horizon momentum/continuation as remaining child orders continue to consume liquidity on the same side.\n\nHowever, not all aggressive imbalances are equally informative: if aggressive spread-crossing orders do not translate into executed trades (low conversion / low fill efficiency, high cancel rate), the observed imbalance may represent fleeting, opportunistic liquidity taking, spoof-like liquidity probing, or temporary queue-jumping that moves quotes but does not represent durable net buying/selling. In that case, the initial price impact is more likely to be transient, followed by short-horizon mean reversion once liquidity replenishes and the order book normalizes.\n\nObservable quantities: (i) SpreadOrder imbalance = (buy_cross - sell_cross)/(buy_cross + sell_cross + eps) within 5 minutes; (ii) volume expansion = log(OrderLine_total_volume/rolling_median(OrderLine_total_volume, N)) or OrderLine/ADV-scaled surge; (iii) conversion/fill efficiency = TradeLine_volume / SpreadOrder_cross_volume (or executed-trade count divided by spread-cross order count), and/or cancellation proxy if available via submitted vs executed metrics (OrderLine vs TradeLine). The signal can be constructed as a nonlinear interaction: continuation strength increases with (imbalance × volume_surge) but is gated/penalized when conversion is low.\n\nTemporal evolution / decay and regimes: The continuation component should decay quickly (minutes to ~1 hour) as the parent meta-order completes; the reversion component should emerge later (tens of minutes to a day) when imbalance was primarily unfilled and thus represented temporary price pressure rather than information. The effect should be strongest in names with thinner depth / higher fragmentation (small- to mid-cap, higher spread, lower displayed depth) and during liquidity-stressed regimes (open/close, news bursts) where order splitting and venue-routing delays are more pronounced; it may weaken in highly liquid mega-caps or during stable midday sessions. Key failure mode: true informed trading can produce high conversion and longer persistence, reducing reversion and extending momentum beyond the expected decay window.",
  "concise_reason": "Aggressive order-flow imbalance with concurrent order-submission surges reflects persistent meta-order execution, creating short-lived continuation.\nWhen aggressive imbalance fails to convert into trades, the move is more likely temporary price pressure and mean-reverts after liquidity replenishment.",
  "concise_observation": "Large spread-crossing buy/sell imbalance plus a sudden spike in total orders predicts near-term continuation, but low aggressive-to-trade conversion predicts subsequent reversal.",
  "concise_justification": "Consistent with order-flow/price-impact microstructure and the empirical price–volume interaction literature linking volume-backed imbalances to short-horizon momentum vs liquidity-driven mean reversion.",
  "concise_knowledge": "If aggressive imbalance persists with high execution conversion during a volume surge, expect short-horizon continuation; when conversion is low, expect faster decay and later mean reversion."
}
