se after de-sizing).'}, {'pattern': 'Second-order dispersion layering (tsstd on a stability field, then tsquantileupmean) underperforms.', 'failure_reason': 'Stacking variability-of-stability with a tail filter increases complexity without improving monotonic predictiveness, consistent with noise compounding from multiple nonlinear/time-series operators.'}, {'pattern': 'Operator choice appears to drive turnover differences without delivering predictive lift (wdirect variants have higher turnover; quantile variants lower turnover but still weak).', 'failure_reason': 'Higher-turnover constructions do not show better IC/Rank_IC, while lower-turnover constructions also fail to generate a robust signal; this indicates the core feature-set has insufficient edge rather than the issue being purely trading frequency.'}], 'parameter_insights': {'preferred_windows': [], 'unstable_ranges': ['Signals built on 24-step changes/autocorrelation combined with 240-step direction aggregation show inconsistent/weak predictiveness and in one case strongly adverse behavior (SellVolume_Stability via tsdelta + wdirect).', '240-step quantile conditioning at 0.8 does not reliably stabilize IC/Rank_IC and often yields low ICIR, suggesting this parameterization is not robust for the current feature families.']}, 'summary': 'No expressions meet the HIGH-PERFORMING criteria. Across the tested stability and cross-source ratio features, neither wdirect nor (bi)tsquantileupmean delivers robust monotonic predictiveness, and long-leg excess Sharpe is broadly negative. The most notable failure mode is delta-based SellVolume_Stability (amplified adverse alignment), while quantile-tail conditioning at (240, 0.8) generally reduces turnover but does not improve signal quality.'}
